devswarm_role.gate_guard	{"v":"devswarm-parent-gate"}
devswarm_role.gate_summary	{"v":"Stop: the DevSwarm Primary gate (decided in the plugin's engine/logic/devswarm-parent-gate logic file): the early exits, the inert and clean passes (loop state removed), the busy and grace advisories, and the neglect block with its loop state, cap, escalation, waiting-on-human lines and wake line; own unread, pending questions, stale verdicts, a parked escalation and every case it cannot reproduce byte for byte defer to the Node gate before anything is written."}
devswarm_role.hooks_dir	{"v":"hooks"}
devswarm_role.id_extra_chars	{"v":"._-"}
devswarm_role.id_placeholder	{"v":"<DEVSWARM_BUILDER_ID>"}
devswarm_role.judge_env	{"v":"ANTIHALL_JUDGE_CHILD"}
devswarm_role.kill_env	{"v":"DISABLE_ANTIHALL_DEVSWARM"}
devswarm_role.msg_base	{"v":"💡 anti-hall · devswarm-comms: anti-hall's shared mesh store is this workspace's ONLY messaging channel for DevSwarm coordination — native hivecontrol send commands (`workspace message-*`) are BLOCKED. Report status: `{cli} heartbeat <id> --summary \"<text>\"`. Direct-message: `{cli} send --to-primary --message-file <path>` (or `--to <meshId>`). Check in: `{cli} roster`, `mesh read` (CONSUMES broadcasts — use `--peek` to check in without consuming), `inbox read-primary <id>` (read-only — then run the `ackCommand` it returns). RESTING state = keep polling the mesh — do not idle silently. Scope: COMMUNICATION ONLY; this never changes your assigned task. BLOCKED ON A DECISION? Never ask the human directly, never halt all work — a question parks ONE sub-task; keep working every other unblocked item. Send via `send --to-primary --question` (message = blocked/options/recommendation/DEFAULT you'll take/deadline). DEFAULT-AND-PROCEED: no reply by deadline -> take that default, proceed, and flag it LOUDLY as an explicit assumption in your report — never silently. Hard-stop ONLY for a destructive/irreversible action: park + report, do not guess a default. Ladder: child -> parent -> human, never child -> human. WHEN MERGED run `{cli} done --summary \"...\"` once; auto-archived after. If you have been idle with no active task for a while, proactively run `{cli} heartbeat <id> --summary \"idle — reassign me a task or archive me\"` so the parent orchestrator's task list stays honest instead of you sitting idle unnoticed."}
devswarm_role.msg_base_parent	{"v":"💡 anti-hall · devswarm-comms: anti-hall's shared mesh store is this workspace's ONLY messaging channel for DevSwarm coordination — native hivecontrol send commands (`workspace message-*`) are BLOCKED. Report status: `{cli} heartbeat <id> --summary \"<text>\"`. Direct-message: `{cli} send --to-primary --message-file <path>` (or `--to <meshId>`). Check in: `{cli} roster`, `mesh read` (CONSUMES broadcasts — use `--peek` to check in without consuming), `inbox read-primary <id>` (read-only — then run the `ackCommand` it returns). RESTING state = keep polling the mesh — do not idle silently. Scope: COMMUNICATION ONLY; this never changes your assigned task. BLOCKED-QUESTION REPLIES: a child's question means YOU decide from the plan context, replying via `send --to <meshId>`; escalate to the human only for a genuine human call (destructive/irreversible, scope-changing, unsafe to assume). Ladder: child -> parent -> human, never child -> human. Full verb list: `{cli} help --short`."}
devswarm_role.msg_expiry_inline	{"v":" + re-arm on its final/expired event"}
devswarm_role.msg_expiry_tick	{"v":". Do NOT re-arm inline when it emits its final/expired event — reply in one line and stop; the next `inbox tick` cron turn re-arms it via `watcherArmed`"}
devswarm_role.msg_wake_claude	{"v":" MAILBOX WAKE (do this NOW, on your FIRST turn): call `CronList`. If ANY existing job already runs `inbox tick` for this workspace — any schedule, id, or partition UUID — that counts as present; do nothing, never create a second one. Only if ABSENT — never created, or auto-expired (recurring tasks self-delete 7 days after creation) — call `CronCreate` with schedule `{cron}` and a prompt that runs run `{cli} inbox tick {id} --child --quiet` (run plain: no pipes/filters) (with `--child` it first imports anything waiting in your native queue, then prints one line with the SAME unread/meshGap/known signal `inbox count` reports, and writes a liveness marker + refreshes your heartbeat — one command instead of pull+count); If its `watcherArmed` reads `false` (your Monitor lapsed at its 30-min cap; not `idle-skip`/`limit-skip`), re-arm: `Monitor` with `{watcher}` and timeout_ms 1800000 (without it the Monitor expires after the 5-minute default; add persistent: true if supported). if the printed line reads `unread 0` AND `meshGap false` AND `known` is NOT `false`, say so and stop — do NOT spawn a subagent; otherwise (either `unread` is greater than 0, or `meshGap` is `true`, or `known` is `false`), run `{cli} inbox read-primary {id}` (read-only; after handling, run the `ackCommand` it returns) (delegate to a subagent only if the payload is large — this is the cursor-advancing verb, matching devswarm-child-turn.js's own mesh-direct instruction; `inbox read` is a non-mutating peek and cannot clear the withheld gap) — if that reports `ok:false`, report the `reason` (and `storeUnavailableReason`/`storeUnavailableDetail` when present) in one line and stop (do not loop, do not spawn a subagent) and acts on anything unread. Cron jobs fire while this session is IDLE (each tick is a full turn) — this cron is now a 30-MINUTE FALLBACK cadence (cheaper than the pre-D13 5-minute default), NEVER disarmed — it is the only thing that will wake you to drain your mailbox once you go idle; a message that lands after you stop is otherwise never seen. ALSO arm the `Monitor` tool as your PRIMARY wake path (Cron above is the 30-minute FALLBACK — it still fires independently; Monitor is IN ADDITION, never instead): first check whether a mailbox-wake Monitor is already armed so you never arm a second one (double-arming causes duplicate wakes; the watcher script also self-guards with a lock, but do not rely on that alone). If none is armed, call `Monitor` with command `{watcher}` and a description like \"devswarm mailbox wake watcher\": use `persistent: true` if your Monitor tool supports it, else max `timeout_ms`{expiry} — never two watchers at once. Every line it prints on stdout becomes a transcript event, waking this session even while fully idle, with far lower latency than the cron tick above. A first line starting `[wake-watch] REFUSED TO ARM` means you do NOT have this coverage — do not assume you do; the cron job above is still your only wake path."}
devswarm_role.msg_wake_claude_parent	{"v":" MAILBOX WAKE (do this NOW, on your FIRST turn): call `CronList`. If ANY existing job already runs `inbox tick` for this workspace — any schedule, id, or partition UUID — that counts as present; do nothing, never create a second one. Only if ABSENT — never created, or auto-expired (recurring tasks self-delete 7 days after creation) — call `CronCreate` with schedule `{cron}` and a prompt that runs run `{cli} inbox tick {id} --quiet` (run plain: no pipes/filters) (with `--child` it first imports anything waiting in your native queue, then prints a status line with the SAME unread/meshGap/known signal `inbox count` reports, and writes a liveness marker + refreshes your heartbeat — one command instead of pull+count); If its `watcherArmed` reads `false` (your Monitor lapsed at its 30-min cap; not `idle-skip`/`limit-skip`), re-arm: `Monitor` with `{watcher}` and timeout_ms 1800000 (without it the Monitor expires after the 5-minute default; add persistent: true if supported). if the FIRST printed line (any lines after it are an informational roster: ignore them here) reads `unread 0` AND `meshGap false` AND `known` is NOT `false`, say so and stop — do NOT spawn a subagent; otherwise (either `unread` is greater than 0, or `meshGap` is `true`, or `known` is `false`), run `{cli} inbox read-primary {id}` (read-only; after handling, run the `ackCommand` it returns) (delegate to a subagent only if the payload is large) — if that reports `ok:false`, report the `reason` (and `storeUnavailableReason`/`storeUnavailableDetail` when present) in one line and stop (do not loop, do not spawn a subagent) and acts on anything unread. Cron jobs fire while this session is IDLE (each tick is a full turn) — this cron is now a 30-MINUTE FALLBACK cadence (cheaper than the pre-D13 5-minute default), NEVER disarmed — it is the only thing that will wake you to drain your mailbox once you go idle; a message that lands after you stop is otherwise never seen. ALSO arm the `Monitor` tool as your PRIMARY wake path (Cron above is the 30-minute FALLBACK — it still fires independently; Monitor is IN ADDITION, never instead): first check whether a mailbox-wake Monitor is already armed so you never arm a second one (double-arming causes duplicate wakes; the watcher script also self-guards with a lock, but do not rely on that alone). If none is armed, call `Monitor` with command `{watcher}` and a description like \"devswarm mailbox wake watcher\": use `persistent: true` if your Monitor tool supports it, else max `timeout_ms`{expiry} — never two watchers at once. Every line it prints on stdout becomes a transcript event, waking this session even while fully idle, with far lower latency than the cron tick above. A first line starting `[wake-watch] REFUSED TO ARM` means you do NOT have this coverage — do not assume you do; the cron job above is still your only wake path."}
devswarm_role.msg_wake_other	{"v":" MAILBOX WAKE: this workspace runs `{agent}`, which has NO idle-wake primitive — once you go idle, nothing can wake you, so a message that lands after you stop waits for your next turn. Drain your mailbox at the START of every turn and again BEFORE you stop: first run `{cli} inbox pull {id}` (cheap, inline — imports anything waiting in your native queue) then `{cli} inbox count {id}` (both plain: no pipes/filters); if `unreadTotal` is 0 AND `meshGapWithheld` is NOT `true` AND `known` is NOT `false`, say so and stop — do NOT spawn a subagent; otherwise (either `unreadTotal` is greater than 0, or `meshGapWithheld` is `true`, or `known` is `false`), run `{cli} inbox read-primary {id}` (read-only; after handling, run the `ackCommand` it returns) (delegate to a subagent only if the payload is large — its ackCommand is the cursor-advancing step, matching devswarm-child-turn.js's own mesh-direct instruction; `inbox read` is a non-mutating peek and cannot clear the withheld gap) — if that reports `ok:false`, report the `reason` (and `storeUnavailableReason`/`storeUnavailableDetail` when present) in one line and stop (do not loop, do not spawn a subagent)."}
devswarm_role.msg_wake_other_parent	{"v":" MAILBOX WAKE: this workspace runs `{agent}`, which has NO idle-wake primitive — once you go idle, nothing can wake you, so a message that lands after you stop waits for your next turn. Drain your mailbox at the START of every turn and again BEFORE you stop: first run `{cli} inbox count {id}` (run plain: no pipes/filters); if `unreadTotal` is 0 AND `meshGapWithheld` is NOT `true` AND `known` is NOT `false`, say so and stop — do NOT spawn a subagent; otherwise (either `unreadTotal` is greater than 0, or `meshGapWithheld` is `true`, or `known` is `false`), run `{cli} inbox read-primary {id}` (read-only; after handling, run the `ackCommand` it returns) (delegate to a subagent only if the payload is large) — if that reports `ok:false`, report the `reason` (and `storeUnavailableReason`/`storeUnavailableDetail` when present) in one line and stop (do not loop, do not spawn a subagent)."}
